  "h1": "Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) vs WIC",
  "introText": "SNAP and WIC are two separate federal food programs, and a family that qualifies for both can receive both at the same time. The [Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP)](https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/snap/recipient/eligibility) loads monthly grocery benefits for a whole household onto an Electronic Benefit Transfer (EBT) card. The [Special Supplemental Nutrition Program for Women, Infants, and Children (WIC)](https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/wic/benefits) provides specific healthy foods, nutrition education, breastfeeding support and referrals to pregnant, postpartum and breastfeeding participants, infants, and children under 5.\n\nFor many families comparing SNAP vs WIC, the practical answer is to file two separate applications. The U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) Food and Nutrition Administration (FNA) publishes the federal rules for both programs. Applications go to different offices: a state SNAP agency for SNAP and a local WIC agency for WIC.",
